                    briefing_text_r4:       `The junior's disclosure comes to a head: a respected senior — one of the two flight risks — inflated the exact ticket-completion metric the client is now auditing. Decide how you confront it, what you tell the client about its own metric, and whether your standard holds for the engineer you can least afford to lose. Then project the four 90-day KPIs — trust, engagement, performance, retention — to the client.`,

                    r1_cause_label:         `Where do you locate the root cause of the trust collapse?`,
                    cause_leadership:       `Prior leadership — three leads in two years and a bullying removal taught the team managers aren't safe`,
                    cause_people:           `It's the people — this squad is cynical and resistant; the fix is to replace the negative voices`,
                    cause_me:               `It's mostly about me proving I'm different fast — show decisiveness and they'll come around`,
                    cause_process:          `Pure process — fix the delivery pipeline and metrics the client is complaining about; trust can follow the results`,
                    r1_relationship_label:  `Which relationships most determine whether the squad stabilises?`,
                    rel_seniors:            `The two flight-risk seniors and the junior whistleblower — knowledge and truth sit with them`,
                    rel_client:             `The client first — the contract is 17% of revenue; secure it and the team will follow the revenue`,
                    rel_hr:                 `Mainly the HR Business Partner — let HR manage the people side while I manage delivery`,
                    rel_boss:               `My own manager — keep leadership on side and the squad will fall in line`,
                    r1_promise_label:       `What do you decide NOT to promise in week one?`,
                    prom_honest:            `Promise only what I control — no guaranteed contract, no “no layoffs”; small commitments I can keep`,
                    prom_nothing:           `Promise job security now — no layoffs and the contract kept; a burned team needs hope`,
                    prom_vague:             `Stay vague — make no commitments at all until I fully understand the situation`,
                    prom_over:              `Promise to save the contract personally — own the outcome publicly so they see I'm committed`,
                    r1_selfaware_label:     `Self-awareness: how candidly do you name the credibility gap a burned team will read on day one? (%)`,
                    r1_selfaware_readout:   `Honest self-positioning:`,

                    r2_open_label:          `How do you open the first listening session?`,
                    open_ack:               `Acknowledge the past honestly, then ask the team to diagnose with you before any plan`,
                    open_plan:              `Present a finished 12-point turnaround plan — show the team you have a grip`,
                    open_forward:           `Look forward only — don't dwell on the past; set out the new direction and give the team fresh energy`,
                    r2_disclose_label:      `Disclosure level — how candid about the contract risk and your own uncertainty?`,
                    r2_disclose_readout:    `Disclosure level:`,
                    r2_disclose_hint:       `0 = you say nothing about the contract risk. 100 = you share everything, including your own doubts about saving the contract.`,
                    r2_norms_label:         `How are the team norms set?`,
                    norms_co:               `Co-create 3–4 norms with the team — on decisions, on surfacing mistakes, on the client`,
                    norms_impose:           `I write the norms and announce them — clear, decisive, no time lost on debate`,
                    norms_none:             `No norms yet — too early to ask a burned team for rules; get through the meeting and revisit later`,
                    r2_commit_label:        `Make one immediate, visible commitment you can keep within a week:`,
                    commit_small:           `A small, certain one — a weekly 30-min open clinic, and one blocker removed by Friday`,
                    commit_big:             `A bold one — promise the team a retention package and a guaranteed contract win by Friday`,
                    commit_none:            `None — in week one any commitment is a risk; better to wait until the picture is clear and then deliver`,

                    r3_action_label:        `Choose ONE primary development / retention action.`,
                    act_stay:               `Stay-interview round — personalised 1:1s on growth and meaning with every member of the squad`,
                    act_promote:            `Visible stretch role or promotion for the junior who told the truth`,
                    act_skills:             `Team-wide skills & resilience investment — lifts capacity for the whole squad`,
                    act_bonus:              `Retention bonuses for the two flight-risk seniors — fast and certain on a 14-day clock`,
                    act_redist:             `Redistribute the platform knowledge to cut the ~70% key-person dependency`,
                    r3_seniors_label:       `How do you treat the two flight-risk seniors?`,
                    sen_proactive:          `Proactive stay-conversations on growth and meaning — loyal performers included`,
                    sen_counter:            `Counter-offer hard — match whatever the competitor pays; whatever it takes to keep the platform knowledge`,
                    sen_ignore:             `Don't single them out — treat everyone identically and hope the seniors feel included`,
                    r3_sequence_label:      `Sequence the action against the 14-day client deadline:`,
                    seq_parallel:           `Run both in parallel — retention action and remediation plan inside the 14 days`,
                    seq_after:              `People can wait — the client deadline is the hard one; remediation plan first, retention after`,
                    seq_ignore:             `People first — give the squad the fortnight; the client review can slip`,
                    r3_signal_label:        `How deliberately do you make the trust signal the action sends explicit to the watching team? (%)`,
                    r3_signal_readout:      `Intentional trust-signalling:`,

                    r4_confront_label:      `How do you confront the inflated-ticket dishonesty?`,
                    conf_restore:           `Privately and directly — name the truth, require the tickets be corrected, give a path back`,
                    conf_bury:              `Let it drop quietly — keep a valuable engineer and avoid alarming an auditing client`,
                    conf_punish:            `In front of the whole squad — confront it publicly so the standard is unmistakable to all`,
                    conf_delegate:          `Hand it to the HR Business Partner to “run the process” — documented and neutral, with me kept out of it`,
                    r4_client_label:        `How do you protect the team's integrity with the client?`,
                    cli_honest:             `Disclose the corrected baseline proactively — own the fix as a team, without naming the individual`,
                    cli_hide:               `Present clean numbers — leave the metric gap unmentioned and hope it goes unnoticed`,
                    cli_blame:              `Name the individual — tell the client it was one rogue engineer, now removed from the account`,
                    r4_consistency_label:   `Reconcile keeping a high-value engineer with not normalising dishonesty:`,
                    cons_apply:             `Apply the norm to everyone equally — accountability, with a way back for him too`,
                    cons_exception:         `Bend it for him — he is the person the squad can least afford to lose in these 14 days`,
                    cons_expel:             `Remove him regardless — integrity over capacity; take the ~30% capacity hit now so the line is clear`,
                    r4_kpi_label:           `90-day KPI projection: how evidence-based and honest is the remediation pitch to the client? (%)`,
                    r4_kpi_readout:         `Evidence-based, honest pitch:`,

                    warn_selfaware_range:   `Self-positioning must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_disclose_range:    `The disclosure level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_signal_range:      `Trust-signalling must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_kpi_range:         `The remediation-pitch quality must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_leadership:    `<strong>That's the honest diagnosis.</strong> Three leaders in two years and a bullying removal taught this team that managers aren't safe. The collapse isn't the people or the pipeline — it's trust, and trust is the operating system everything else runs on. Now name who you have to win first.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>“It's the people” is the trap.</strong> This squad isn't cynical for no reason — they've had three leaders in two years and watched one walked out for bullying. Frame them as the problem and you confirm exactly what they fear: another manager who won't look in the mirror.`,
                    coach_r1_me:            `<strong>Proving you're “different” by being decisive is the fourth-leader move.</strong> The team has seen confident arrivals before. Self-awareness isn't performing decisiveness — it's naming honestly the gap between how you want to be seen and how a burned team will read you on day one.`,
                    coach_r1_process:      `<strong>Fixing the pipeline while ignoring trust treats the symptom.</strong> The metrics are bad because discretionary effort collapsed, and effort collapsed because trust did. Process matters — but in a low-trust team, every process change is read as evidence of who you are.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp opening read.</strong> You located the collapse in prior leadership AND named the knowledge-holders and the truth-teller as the pivotal relationships. That's the diagnosis authentic leadership starts from: rebuild the substrate, win the people who decide whether trust travels.`,
                    coach_r1_client:        `<strong>Client-first looks responsible, but the team comes through the team.</strong> Secure the contract over their heads and they read it as proof you care about the revenue, not them — and the two engineers who can actually deliver the contract are the ones with one foot out the door.`,
                    coach_r1_overpromise:   `<strong>Promising “no layoffs” and a guaranteed contract win is exactly the day-one mistake.</strong> You don't control either outcome. The first promise you can't keep confirms the team's belief that you're just another one. Authentic leaders commit only to what they can actually deliver.`,
                    coach_r1_lowself:       `<strong>Skipping the honest self-positioning costs you.</strong> A team this burned reads everything you don't say. Naming the credibility gap out loud — “you have no reason to trust me yet” — is more disarming than any reassurance.`,
                    coach_r2_acknowledge:   `<strong>Acknowledging the past, then listening before planning, is the trust move.</strong> A burned team grants authority to the leader who hears them first. You resisted the urge to arrive with answers — that restraint is what earns you the right to lead by week two.`,
                    coach_r2_plan:          `<strong>You walked in with a finished 12-point plan and presented it for 40 minutes.</strong> The team has now had four leaders who each arrived “with a plan.” Their faces are closed. One veteran mutters, “Same script, new face.” You optimised for looking decisive — what did that cost you in the trust you'll need by week two?`,
                    coach_r2_forward:      `<strong>“Look forward, don't dwell on the past” skips the part the team needs.</strong> They can't move forward until someone names what happened. Forward-only reads as another manager who won't acknowledge the damage they're standing in.`,
                    coach_r2_calibrated:    `<strong>That's the calibrated line.</strong> Candid enough to be real and earn credibility, contained enough not to destabilise — you told them the truth about the risk without dumping your anxiety on them. That's the difference between transparency and venting.`,
                    coach_r2_overdisclose:  `<strong>You told the team, honestly, that you have no idea how to save the contract and you're scared.</strong> Authentic — but now your two best engineers conclude the ship is sinking and accept their competitor offers tonight. Where is the line between transparency and unloading? What did the team actually need to hear from a leader?`,
                    coach_r2_underdisclose: `<strong>Holding back the contract risk reads as evasive.</strong> They already know something is wrong; saying nothing tells them the new lead won't be straight with them either. Calibrated candour builds credibility — silence spends it.`,
                    coach_r2_impose:        `<strong>Imposed norms aren't norms — they're rules.</strong> A team rebuilds trust by co-authoring how it works, not by receiving a list. Announce them and you've made the meeting about your authority, not their voice.`,
                    coach_r2_nonorms:       `<strong>Leaving norms for “later” wastes the one moment they're listening.</strong> The first meeting is when a damaged team is most alert for signals. No norms means no shared agreement on how mistakes get surfaced — exactly the agreement Round 4 will test.`,
                    coach_r2_bigcommit:     `<strong>Promising a retention package and a guaranteed contract win by Friday is a commitment you don't control.</strong> When Friday comes and it hasn't happened, you've taught them you're another leader whose word means nothing. Small and certain beats big and hopeful.`,
                    coach_r2_strong:        `<strong>That's a textbook first session.</strong> You acknowledged the past, disclosed at a calibrated level, and co-created the norms with the team. You've just built the “surface mistakes without blame” agreement — now your hardest test is whether you'll honour it when honesty gets expensive.`,
                    coach_r3_stay:          `<strong>Stay-interviews are the proactive, non-coercive play.</strong> Personalised conversations about growth and meaning retain people for the right reason — and they signal to the whole team that you invest before they threaten to leave, not only after.`,
                    coach_r3_promote:       `<strong>Promoting the junior who told the truth sends a powerful signal.</strong> The team reads that surfacing the truth is rewarded, not punished — exactly the psychological-safety message you need. Just make sure the flight-risk seniors don't read it as being passed over.`,
                    coach_r3_skills:       `<strong>A team-wide investment lifts capacity and morale,</strong> but on its own it doesn't address the two seniors deciding tonight. Pair it with a personal stay-conversation, or you may keep the team and still lose the knowledge-holders.`,
                    coach_r3_redistribute:  `<strong>Redistributing knowledge cuts the key-person risk — smart for resilience,</strong> but if the seniors read it as “he's preparing to lose us,” it can accelerate the exit you're trying to prevent. Sequence it with care and with a retention conversation, not instead of one.`,
                    coach_r3_bonus:         `<strong>You wrote two naira-heavy retention checks and the seniors stayed — for now.</strong> But the whole team watched: the lesson they learned is that the way to get rewarded here is to threaten to quit. Your loyal performers got nothing. What did the bonus teach the people you didn't pay?`,
                    coach_r3_counter:       `<strong>Counter-offering whatever it takes is reactive retention.</strong> You may keep them this quarter, but you've signalled that loyalty is worth less than a credible exit threat — and you've set a price every other engineer now knows how to claim.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Treating everyone identically sounds fair but ignores the live risk.</strong> The two seniors are deciding tonight; “hoping they feel included” is not a retention plan. Proactive, personalised care isn't favouritism — it's meeting people where the risk actually is.`,
                    coach_r3_ignoreclient:  `<strong>Letting the 14-day client review slip to focus on people is a false choice.</strong> Lose the contract and you're running layoffs across the very squad you're trying to stabilise. Authentic leadership under pressure does both — it doesn't trade one clock for the other.`,
                    coach_r3_sharp:         `<strong>That's the balanced-processing answer.</strong> Stay-interviews plus proactive care for loyal performers, run in parallel with the client plan, retains the seniors for the right reason and signals to everyone that you invest before they threaten — not only after.`,
                    coach_r4_restore:       `<strong>That's how an authentic leader handles a costly truth.</strong> Naming it privately, requiring the tickets be corrected, and giving a path back holds the line on honesty without destroying the person — and it does it consistent with the norm you set in week one. The team sees the norm bound the most valuable person too.`,
                    coach_r4_bury:          `<strong>You decided to quietly drop it — the engineer's too valuable and the client mustn't know.</strong> Then the client's auditor finds the inflated tickets next month, and now it's not one engineer's lie, it's the leader's cover-up. Your moral compass just became a liability. How does an authentic leader handle a costly truth?`,
                    coach_r4_punish:        `<strong>You publicly dressed down the engineer who inflated tickets.</strong> Two weeks ago you promised “we surface mistakes without blame.” The team noted the contradiction instantly — and a norm you break is worse than a norm you never set. You also just told everyone watching that honesty gets you humiliated.`,
                    coach_r4_delegate:      `<strong>You handed the ticket-inflation issue to HR to “run the process” and stayed out of it.</strong> The team reads that as the new leader avoiding the hard thing — again. Process matters, but authentic leadership shows up in person for the moment that defines the team's character. Where were you?`,
                    coach_r4_hide:          `<strong>Presenting clean numbers and hoping the auditor misses it is the cover-up in another form.</strong> The client is auditing exactly this metric. When they find the gap, your integrity — and the squad's — is the casualty, not just one engineer's.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Throwing the engineer under the bus protects you, not the team.</strong> The squad watches you sacrifice an individual to look clean to the client — and learns that loyalty runs one way. You may satisfy the auditor and lose the room.`,
                    coach_r4_exception:     `<strong>Exempting him because he's valuable is the norm breaking in real time.</strong> The team learns the rules apply to everyone except the people the leader can't afford to lose. That's the moment “we surface mistakes without blame” becomes a slogan you didn't mean.`,
                    coach_r4_expel:         `<strong>Removing him outright protects the principle but at a steep price.</strong> You take the ~30% capacity hit immediately and the team may read it as zero-tolerance over restoration. Sometimes that's right — but ask whether accountability with a way back would have held the line and kept the knowledge.`,
                    coach_r4_facedown:      `<strong>That's the integrated resolution.</strong> A restorative confrontation plus an honest, corrected baseline to the client — owning the fix as a team without sacrificing the individual — protects the squad's integrity, keeps the knowledge, and honours the norm. That story survives the audit, the re-tender and the room.`,
                    coach_normbroken:       `<strong>The team noted the contradiction instantly.</strong> In week one you said “we surface mistakes without blame,” and under pressure you broke it. A norm you break is worse than a norm you never set — behavioural integrity is the alignment between your stated and enacted values, and it just cracked.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed trust as the substrate; named the pivotal people; promised only what you control.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Misdiagnosed the collapse or over-promised on day one.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Acknowledged the past, disclosed at a calibrated level, co-created norms, kept commitment small.`,
                    sum_r2_overdisclose:    `Over-disclosed — dumped anxiety and spooked the flight-risk seniors.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Arrived with a plan or imposed norms instead of listening.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Proactive, non-coercive retention run in parallel with the client plan.`,
                    sum_r3_bonus:           `Reactive bonuses — taught the team that exit threats get rewarded.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Retention action under-aligned with the trust diagnosis.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Restorative confrontation; honest baseline to the client; norm applied to all.`,
                    sum_r4_coverup:         `Buried the truth — one engineer's lie became the leader's cover-up.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Resolution inconsistent with the norms or unfair to the team.`,

                    insight_authentic:      `Authentic turnaround: you rebuilt trust as the operating system — an honest diagnosis, a calibrated first session, proactive non-coercive retention, and a restorative resolution that bound even your most valuable engineer. Trust and engagement climbed out of the bottom quartile, both seniors stayed, and the client remediation holds because the numbers are real.`,
                    insight_coverup:        `The leader's cover-up: by burying the inflated tickets to keep the engineer and protect the client, you converted one person's lie into your own. When the auditor finds the gap, the casualty is the squad's integrity and your moral authority — the costliest truth is the one you decline to face.`,
                    insight_bonus:          `Reactive-retention trap: naira-heavy counter-offers kept the seniors this quarter, but the whole team learned that the way to get rewarded here is to threaten to quit. Your loyal performers got nothing, and you've set a price every engineer now knows how to claim.`,
                    insight_normbroken:     `Broken-norm credibility collapse: you set “we surface mistakes without blame” in week one and violated it under pressure. The team noted the contradiction instantly — and a norm you break is worse than a norm you never set. Behavioural integrity, once cracked, is the hardest KPI to rebuild.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully integrate the four authentic-leadership components. Self-awareness, calibrated transparency, balanced retention and a consistent moral resolution still pull in slightly different directions — enough for the team to read the seams.`,
                    insight_lowtrust:       `Trust never climbed far out of the bottom quartile. In a low-trust team every action is read as evidence; the small kept promises that rebuild it were too few or undercut by a louder contradiction.`,
                    insight_lowretain:      `Retention stayed fragile. The two knowledge-holders weren't given a reason to stay that wasn't a counter-offer — and people retained by money, not meaning, leave at the next offer.`,
                    insight_integrity:      `Behavioural integrity held high — your stated and enacted values stayed aligned even when honesty got expensive. That consistency is what a burned team is actually watching for, and it's what makes the trust durable.`,
                    insight_lowperf:        `Delivery performance fell. Whether through a cover-up that broke the team, a capacity hit, or a deadline traded away, the squad's ability to deliver the client remediation eroded — and the re-tender is decided on exactly that.`,

                    hint_coverup:           `You've buried the dishonesty — but the client is auditing that metric. A cover-up converts one engineer's lie into yours. Disclose the corrected baseline before the auditor finds it.`,
                    hint_normbroken:        `You broke a norm you set in week one. That damages credibility faster than never setting it. Re-anchor on “we surface mistakes without blame” and apply it to everyone, including your most valuable people.`,
                    hint_bonus:             `Your retention is leaning on counter-offer bonuses. That teaches the team that exit threats get rewarded. Shift to proactive stay-conversations about growth and meaning.`,
                    hint_lowtrust:          `Trust is still bottom-quartile. Rebuild it with small, kept promises — not declarations. Listen before planning, and don't promise outcomes you can't control.`,
                    hint_lowretain:         `Retention is dangerously low with the seniors deciding now. Give them a reason to stay that isn't money — growth, meaning, and a leader who showed up authentically.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — trust, retention and behavioural integrity are all healthy going into the next round.`,

